  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Use mathematical induction to prove each of the following for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$, except in part (d), where $n\\ge2$:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q2a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\\cdot i!=(n+1)!-1$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nCheck $n=1$; add $(k+1)(k+1)!$ to the assumed sum and factor out $(k+1)!$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, the left-hand side is $1\\cdot1!=1$ and the right-hand side is $2!-1=1$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i\\cdot i!=(k+1)!-1$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i\\cdot i!&=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i\\cdot i!+(k+1)(k+1)!\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i\\cdot i!&=(k+1)!-1+(k+1)(k+1)!\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i\\cdot i!&=(k+1)![1+(k+1)]-1\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i\\cdot i!&=(k+2)!-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q2b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{i-1}{i!}=\\frac{n!-1}{n!}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nCheck $n=1$; use the inductive hypothesis and a common denominator of $(k+1)!$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, both sides are $0$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{i-1}{i!}=\\frac{k!-1}{k!}$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{i-1}{i!}&=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{i-1}{i!}+\\frac{k}{(k+1)!}\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{i-1}{i!}&=\\frac{k!-1}{k!}+\\frac{k}{(k+1)!}\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{i-1}{i!}&=\\frac{(k+1)(k!-1)+k}{(k+1)!}\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{i-1}{i!}&=\\frac{(k+1)!-1}{(k+1)!}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q2c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i^3=\\frac{n^2(n+1)^2}{4}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nAfter adding $(k+1)^3$, factor out $(k+1)^2$ and use $k^2+4k+4=(k+2)^2$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, $1^3=1=\\frac{1^2(1+1)^2}{4}$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i^3=\\frac{k^2(k+1)^2}{4}$ for some $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i^3&=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i^3+(k+1)^3\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i^3&=\\frac{k^2(k+1)^2}{4}+(k+1)^3\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i^3&=\\frac{(k+1)^2[k^2+4(k+1)]}{4}\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i^3&=\\frac{(k+1)^2(k^2+4k+4)}{4}\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1}i^3&=\\frac{(k+1)^2(k+2)^2}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the formula holds for $k+1$. By mathematical induction, it holds for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 106
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q2d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$$\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\frac{4}{i^2-1}=\\frac{(n-1)(3n+2)}{n(n+1)},\\qquad n\\ge2$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True for all integers $n\\ge2$.\n\nStart at $n=2$; add $\\frac4{k(k+2)}$ and combine over $k(k+1)(k+2)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=2$, the left-hand side is $\\frac4{2^2-1}=\\frac43$ and the right-hand side is $\\frac{(2-1)(3\\cdot2+2)}{2(2+1)}=\\frac43$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $\\sum_{i=2}^{k}\\frac4{i^2-1}=\\frac{(k-1)(3k+2)}{k(k+1)}$ for some $k\\ge2$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{i=2}^{k+1}\\frac4{i^2-1}&=\\sum_{i=2}^{k}\\frac4{i^2-1}+\\frac4{(k+1)^2-1}\\\\\\sum_{i=2}^{k+1}\\frac4{i^2-1}&=\\frac{(k-1)(3k+2)}{k(k+1)}+\\frac4{k(k+2)}\\\\\\sum_{i=2}^{k+1}\\frac4{i^2-1}&=\\frac{(k-1)(3k+2)(k+2)+4(k+1)}{k(k+1)(k+2)}\\\\\\sum_{i=2}^{k+1}\\frac4{i^2-1}&=\\frac{3k^3+5k^2}{k(k+1)(k+2)}\\\\\\sum_{i=2}^{k+1}\\frac4{i^2-1}&=\\frac{k(3k+5)}{(k+1)(k+2)}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe last expression is the required right-hand side for $n=k+1$. By mathematical induction, the formula holds for all integers $n\\ge2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 152
      }
    ]
  },
